To run C code using Notepad++, you need to follow these steps:

1. Write Your C Code
Open Notepad++.

Write or paste your C code in a new file.

Save the file with a .c extension, for example, program.c.

2. Install a C Compiler
You need a C compiler like GCC. On Windows, you can install:

MinGW (Minimalist GNU for Windows)

TDM-GCC

Or use WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux) if you have it installed.

3. Configure Notepad++ to Compile and Run C Code
You can use the NppExec plugin in Notepad++ to compile and run your C programs.

Steps for NppExec Setup:
Open Notepad++, go to the Plugins menu → Plugins Admin.

Search for NppExec and install it.

Restart Notepad++ after installation.

Create a Compile and Run Script:
Go to Plugins → NppExec → Execute….

Enter this script (adjust paths if necessary; here assuming MinGW is installed and gcc is in your PATH):

bash
gcc "$(FULL_CURRENT_PATH)" -o "$(CURRENT_DIRECTORY)\$(NAME_PART).exe"
if $(EXITCODE) == 0
    cmd /c "$(CURRENT_DIRECTORY)\$(NAME_PART).exe"
endif
Click Save and name it e.g., Compile and Run C.

Now, you can run this script from Plugins → NppExec → Execute Script.

4. Run Your Code
Open your .c file.

Use Plugins → NppExec → your saved script (Compile and Run C).

The console inside Notepad++ will show compilation and program output.

Notes:
Make sure gcc is accessible from your command prompt (added to PATH).

You can also set a shortcut for the NppExec script for convenience.

If code has errors, compiler messages will appear in the console.

This way, you can write, compile, and run C code all from Notepad++ efficiently. If you want, I can guide you through installing MinGW or setting PATH variables specifically.
